            {"id": "a", "text": "The laser scanner assembly (polygon mirror motor) is experiencing intermittent bearing failure, causing scan line dropout", "correct": True, "rationale": "Correct. The laser scanner assembly contains a polygon mirror spinning at thousands of RPM. The laser beam reflects off rotating mirror facets to scan each horizontal line across the drum. If the polygon mirror motor bearing is failing, it causes intermittent speed variations or momentary pauses — some scan lines are missed entirely. This produces random 2–3 line dropouts at unpredictable positions, since the failure is intermittent rather than position-dependent. The symptom does not repeat at drum circumference intervals (which would indicate drum defect) and is not consistent (which rules out a static formatter/memory issue)."},
            {"id": "b", "text": "The paper feed rollers are slipping intermittently, causing the paper to advance unevenly", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Intermittent paper feed slippage would cause line spacing variations (some lines too close, some too far apart) or paper skew — not missing lines. Feed roller slippage manifests as dimensional inaccuracy in the output, not absence of printed content."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "The print memory (RAM) in the formatter is failing, causing partial page data loss before transmission to the laser", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. The question states the formatter was tested on another printer and works correctly, ruling out formatter RAM as the cause. Even if formatter RAM were failing, it would typically cause garbled output or incomplete page data, not random 2–3 line dropouts at varying positions."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "The HVPS is providing intermittent voltage to the charging corona, causing uncharged drum areas that fail to attract toner", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Intermittent charging failures would produce random white areas or fading across broader sections of the page — not discrete 2–3 line dropouts. Charging issues affect the entire drum area within the voltage anomaly window, not individual scan lines."},
