            {"id": "a", "text": "USB-C ports without the Thunderbolt icon are USB 3.x/USB4 only and do not carry Thunderbolt protocol signals required by the Thunderbolt 3 audio interface", "correct": True, "rationale": "Correct. USB-C is a physical connector standard. Not all USB-C ports on a system carry Thunderbolt protocol — only ports connected to a Thunderbolt controller (identified by the lightning bolt icon) support Thunderbolt peripherals. Thunderbolt 3/4 devices require Thunderbolt protocol, which includes PCIe tunneling for the audio interface's driver communication. A standard USB-C port (USB 3.x/USB4 without Thunderbolt) cannot enumerate Thunderbolt-only devices. The audio interface must be connected to a Thunderbolt-capable USB-C port."},
            {"id": "b", "text": "Thunderbolt 3 devices are incompatible with Thunderbolt 4 ports due to protocol changes between generations", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Thunderbolt 4 is backward compatible with Thunderbolt 3 devices. A Thunderbolt 3 audio interface will work on a Thunderbolt 4 port. The issue in this scenario is that the port used does not support Thunderbolt at all, not a TB3/TB4 compatibility problem."},
            {"id": "c", "text": "The audio interface requires a driver update before it can be recognized on Thunderbolt 4 systems", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Driver issues would potentially affect functionality after enumeration, but a Thunderbolt device that is not recognized at all (no enumeration) indicates a hardware-level connectivity issue — the port does not support Thunderbolt protocol. Driver issues do not prevent hardware-level device detection."},
            {"id": "d", "text": "The USB-C cable is HDMI Alt Mode only and blocks Thunderbolt data signals", "correct": False, "rationale": "Incorrect. Standard USB-C cables do not carry mode-specific alternative protocols in the cable itself (with the exception of active cables requiring E-marker chips). A standard passive USB-C cable does not 'block' Thunderbolt signals. The issue is the port's controller, not the cable."},
